This is an independent experimental implementation for the Quansheng
UV-K10 Pro. It is not an official Quansheng or Puya release and is not
endorsed by those companies.

The code was written from public hardware documentation and observations of
the official K10PRO_ENG_v8.02.16_publish.bin application and the statically
inspected official programming applications. This is not a claim
of a separated clean-room process. See docs/reverse-engineering.md for the
reference sample hashes, observed addresses, sources, and uncertainty.

No Quansheng firmware image, vendor disassembly, bootloader, external Flash
dump, calibration data, font image, APK, CPS executable, complete vendor JSON
schema, bundled vendor CSV, or Si47xx patch is included. The MIT
license applies to the new project code and documentation, not to third-party
reference materials or trademarks.

The 128-byte firmware-container XOR constant is a protocol interoperability
parameter, also documented by the F4HWN project's tools/serialtool/_fwcodec.py:
https://github.com/armel/uv-k1-k5v3-firmware-custom
The codec implementation in this repository is independently written.

The 16-byte programming-frame XOR constant, command identifiers, field offsets,
observed enum meanings and artifact hashes are interoperability/research facts.
The offline codecs, synthetic data, function-level emulator harness and schema
summarizer were independently authored. A schema field is not a claim that its
vendor formatter or the corresponding radio operation has been implemented.
See docs/emulation.md for explicit hardware replacements and model limitations.

Register definitions are documented by Puya's PY32F071 datasheet and OpenPuya
CMSIS/LL headers. Si47xx command definitions are documented in Skyworks AN332.
See docs/reverse-engineering.md and docs/pinmap.md for source links.
